<p>Engrossing, the link to 4700 is pretty smart, and I really like the way it's an unspoken explanation for the prevalence of celtic knots in bronze age decorations around the North and Irish sea regions.</p>
As ever a couple of small proofing observations:<br />
1/ bit of an odd sentence towards the end of the second log, &quot;various scale pigmentation's&quot;
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>- ignoring the misplaced apostrophe, this would probably scan better as &quot;variously pigmented scales&quot;. Also the gravity is maybe better described as being &quot;at zero&quot; rather than &quot;at 0&quot;, most style guides discourage the use of inline ordinal numbers for small quantities, and with the page formatting as it is - at least on my 1280x800 monitor, in Chrome/Win7 - it ends up as an orphan on its own line. Though the first correction may fix that and the expansion may then reverse the fix,</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
so, YMMV.<br />
<span style="white-space: pre-wrap;">&#32;</span><br />
2/ You have a &quot;.txt&quot; file with images and variable font, justification and divider formatting.
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>TXT is well established as containing plain ASCII text only, and it seems odd that the Foundation would create their own version which varies from this. There are plenty of alternatives that would suffice, however - you could go with the MS standard .DOC or .DOCX (depending how much the Foundation has modernised all its computers), the surprisingly also Microsoft but more &quot;open&quot; .RTF (it's somewhat deprecated these days, though), the OpenOffice .ODT (open document format, text), or even just good old .PDF. Or of course some random bunch of letters signifying an entirely novel Foundation-specific document type, seeing as there's already &quot;.log&quot;s (which are themselves usually plaintext, but it's not exactly a standard and variants exist which e.g. include ODT-like XML tags).</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">edit: what the actual ████ is going on with the formatting of these comments, honestly. &#8212;Can a guy get a line break?! [DATA REDACTED]</span></p>

